  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21359v1</id>
    <title>Limit shape, avalanches, and stabilisation of abelian sandpiles on comb lattices</title>
    <updated>2026-09-18T06:18:19Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.21359v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.21359v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We study two aspects of the abelian sandpile model on comb lattices. First, we prove that the infinite-volume limit of the stationary measures is supported on the saturated configuration. We then investigate the shape of avalanches induced by adding a particle at the origin in finite boxes of size $(2n+1)\times(2n+1)$ around the origin. In the stationary distribution on these finite boxes, we show that avalanches reach the boundary along the vertical teeth with probability tending to $1$, while the horizontal spread is of order $\sqrt{n}$. Finally, we establish that the single-source limit shape for abelian sandpiles on the comb lattice agrees with the corresponding limit shapes for the divisible sandpile, internal diffusion-limited aggregation (IDLA), and rotor-router aggregation models. This establishes limit shape universality on the comb lattice.</summary>
    <category term="math.PR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-09-18T06:18:19Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>26 pages, 5 figures</arxiv:comment>
